# i18n for UI Copy

All user-facing strings in components and routes go through `react-i18next`. Do not hardcode display text (labels, titles, empty states, toasts, pending messages, button copy, errors shown to users).

## When adding or changing UI copy

1. Prefer an **existing** key if one already fits (`src/i18n/resources/en.ts` is the catalog source of truth).
2. If missing, add the key to **`en.ts` first**, then to **every** locale file under `src/i18n/resources/` (`ja`, `ko`, `zhs`, `zht`, `es`, `fr`, `it`, `de`, `pt`, `ru`, `uk`). `engb` reuses `en` — do not add an `engb` file.
3. Update `REQUIRED_KEYS` (and the `LocaleResources` type) in `src/i18n/entityNoun.test.ts` when the new key should be enforced across catalogs.
4. Use `useTranslation("<namespace>")` and `t("key")` in the component.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD
<span>Loading...</span>

// ✅ GOOD — reuse or add common.loading in all locale files
const { t } = useTranslation("common");
<span>{t("loading")}</span>
```

## Namespaces

| Namespace | Use for |
|---|---|
| `common` | Shared UI (loading, language, generic actions/errors) |
| Feature (`home`, `classes`, …) | Copy specific to that area |

Match the namespace to the feature; put cross-cutting strings in `common`.

## Rules of thumb

- Keep values short, non-empty strings in every locale (same key path everywhere).
- `TranslationCatalog` is inferred from `en.ts` — shape changes start there.
- Do not leave English-only keys in other locales when you add a new key; translate or provide a reasonable locale string in the same change.
